meta {
  name: Set the caller's avatar
  type: http
  seq: 5
  tags: [
    authenticated
    write
  ]
}

put {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/users/me/avatar
  body: json
  auth: inherit
}

headers {
  ~Accept-Language: de
  If-Match: {{etag}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "assetId": "{{avatarAssetId}}"
  }
}

script:pre-request {
  if (!bru.getEnvVar("avatarAssetId")) bru.runner.skipRequest(); // this user has no seeded image of that kind
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 200
}

docs {
  Set the caller's avatar (setAvatar)

  Links an uploaded media asset (purpose `AVATAR`) as the avatar. The profile change and a `profile-updated`
  outbox event are written in one transaction (README 4.2).

  Before anything is written, account-service verifies the asset with media-service (`GET /media/{id}`, REST,
  with the caller's own access token forwarded, so media applies its owner check). The asset must exist
  (`media.asset-not-found`, 404), belong to the caller (`media.asset-not-owned`, 403), have the purpose `AVATAR`
  (`media.asset-purpose-mismatch`, 409) and not be `REJECTED` (`media.asset-rejected`, 409). `PROCESSING` is
  accepted, like for wallpapers. While media-service is unreachable the answer is `503`; nothing is changed.

  Access: `authenticated`.
  Uses an image of the active user from the seed (`{{avatarAssetId}}`); skipped for users that have none.
  Error codes: `user.not-found`, `media.asset-not-found`, `media.asset-not-owned`, `media.asset-rejected`, `media.asset-purpose-mismatch`, `request.version-conflict`.
}
